This project has a clear plugin bootstrap and hook registration flow.

Bootstrap and wiring code are high-impact areas and must remain stable.

## Core Rules

- Be careful with plugin initialization order
- Do not introduce breaking changes in bootstrap flow
- Do not assume that all classes in the repository are automatically active

## Active Code Verification

Before building on an existing class, verify whether it is actually used by checking:
- instantiation in bootstrap code
- registration in includes/Core/Plugin.php
- register_hooks() usage
- WordPress add_action / add_filter attachment paths

Treat inactive or commented-out code as non-authoritative.

## Responsibilities

Bootstrap code should handle:
- plugin setup
- constants / configuration wiring
- service instantiation
- hook registration
- integration wiring

Bootstrap code should NOT become a dumping ground for business logic.

## Hook Registration

- Prefer explicit hook registration in dedicated classes
- Keep wiring readable and centralized
- Avoid scattering the same concern across multiple bootstrap locations
- Reuse existing hook containers where possible

## Constants / Configuration

- Avoid adding new hardcoded secrets, tokens or credentials
- Do not expand insecure constant-based secret storage patterns
- Prefer indirection through configuration layers when possible

## Stability

Be extra careful with changes affecting:
- plugin load order
- conditional class loading
- AJAX-only initialization
- admin vs frontend initialization
- WooCommerce-dependent initialization
- email hooks
- checkout/cart related integrations

## Architecture

- Keep bootstrap small and orchestration-focused
- Put functional logic into dedicated classes
- Do not duplicate registration logic across files
- Preserve backward compatibility unless explicitly instructed otherwise

## Change Strategy

Before changing bootstrap:
1. Identify what is currently instantiated
2. Verify which hooks are currently active
3. Confirm whether the target class is active or dead code
4. Make the smallest safe change possible

## Critical Reminder

Small bootstrap mistakes can disable entire plugin features.

Be conservative and explicit.